name    dm
prog    dm
# Disk Master 1.4, a two-pane browser: the directory on the left, a file
# information panel on the right, the disk's own figures below it.  It runs
# the commands on its bottom line through system(), so SHELL has to name a
# shell that can carry them out -- that is what `sh' is here for, and the
# card's caption says the same.  Nothing below types one of those commands:
# what is asserted is that it draws, that its help is reachable, and that
# `q' gets you back out, which is the part a sweep cannot see.
#
# THE KEYS ARE LOWERCASE, though the menu line along the bottom prints them
# capitalised -- `Attr Copy Rename ... Help Quit Shell'.  Capital `H' does
# nothing at all, twice over with nine seconds to answer in; lowercase `h'
# lists the help file.  That is worth knowing before deciding the program
# ignores a key.
rate    0.8
size    24 80
setup   export SHELL=/dd/CMDS/sh
wait    10
snap    panes
key     h
wait    9
snap    help
key     q
wait    3
key     q
wait    5
snap    back
expect  Disk Master
expect  File Information
expect  Press the down arrow
